.he "comm (1)                     9/30/26                     comm (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
comm
- print lines in common
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
comm [ -123 ] file1 [ file2 ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Comm reads two sorted files and writes three columns: the lines
unique to file1, the lines unique to file2, and the lines common to
both.  Column 1 has no indent, column 2 one tab, and column 3 two
tabs.
.sp
The second file may be left off, and then the standard input is read
in its place.  A file named - is also the standard input.
.sp
The columns are not padded to a width.  What is printed is the line
itself with the indent that belongs to its column, so a suppressed
column takes its indent with it: a line printed in column 2 because
column 1 is suppressed has one tab in front of it and not two.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-1	Do not print column 1, the lines unique to file1.

.ti -10
-2	Do not print column 2, the lines unique to file2.

.ti -10
-3	Do not print column 3, the lines common to both files.

.in -10
.sp
The flags may be run together, so -123 prints nothing, -12 prints
only the lines common to both files, and -13 prints only the lines
unique to file2.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
.in +5
.br
.bd
comm -13 dictionary words
.sp
Print the lines of words that are not in dictionary, using comm to
pick one side out of two sorted files.
.in -5
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Both files must be sorted, and on the same collating sequence, or
the columns do not come out in order.  There is no check for it.
.sp
The 1982 micronix /bin/comm is not this program.  It calls itself
common, takes a -wN flag beside the three column flags, and its
output does not match the page: the lines unique to the second file
are dropped.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
diff (1), sort (1), unique (1)
